# Accepted gaps for 29-type-literal-callable-member. A NEW missing edge fails the suite;
# a line here that STARTS working also fails, so the debt cannot rot.
#
# SIX LINES, THREE DEFECTS. The other three resolve correctly and appear here only
# because the two sides name the same declaration differently — recorded so the count is
# not mistaken for six gaps.
#
# ── the three real ones: a function-valued PROPERTY in a TYPE LITERAL ────────
# The engine emits no target. `field_is_callable` needs the field in scope and then its
# type's call signature; a TYPE_LITERAL_* member's owner points at a ts_type_reference
# row rather than a ts_type one (field_decl's own comment says why: an anonymous
# `{ foo(): string }` has members and no declaration to own them), and the property path
# does not bridge that where the method path does.
#
# The matrix in the source is what attributes it: the intersection's object half
# resolves, a METHOD in a type literal resolves, and the same function-valued property on
# an INTERFACE resolves. So it is neither intersections (#147, closed as not
# reproducible), nor aliases, nor the optional call.
members#literalPropertyAliased(LiteralProperty) -> members#<arrow@47>(number)
members#literalPropertyInline({ run: (n: number) =) -> members#<arrow@53>(number)
members#literalPropertyOptional(OptionalLiteralProperty) -> members#<arrow@57>(number)
#
# ── the three label divergences: the engine RESOLVES these ───────────────────
# Same declaration, same line, different owner name — and in opposite directions, which
# is why neither side is simply right:
#
#   literalMethod       engine `{ run(n: number): string }#run`  oracle `members#run`
#   objectHalfMethod    engine `{ orig...; meta: Meta; }#orig`   oracle `members#orig`
#   interfaceProperty   engine `members#<arrow@39>`              oracle `Holder#<arrow@39>`
#
# The engine names an anonymous type literal by its text and the oracle by the module;
# for an arrow that is an interface property's type the oracle names the interface and
# the engine the module. Same class as #299, where the case oracle named a namespace
# member by its file — it understates accuracy rather than measuring it. Filed separately;
# these lines move to resolved when that lands.
members#literalMethod(LiteralMethod) -> members#run(number)
members#objectHalfMethod(Runner) -> members#orig(number)
members#interfaceProperty(Holder) -> Holder#<arrow@39>(number)
